  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1244 AM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nNot much change to the forecast thinking for the extended\nperiod. The upper high retrogrades west, while an upper longwave\ntrough moves across the northern states by early next week.\n\nFriday...the upper high begins to shift west across the\nIntermountain West towards the Great Basin. This will shift the\nupper pattern, putting northerly flow across Eastern Colorado.\nAn embedded wave is forecast to drop south by the afternoon,\nwith another round of thunderstorms for the area.\n\nLow level surface flow on Friday will turn east to southeasterly\nhelping keep moisture pooled across the Plains. Shear increases\nby the afternoon, and around 1000 j/kg of SBCAPE are expected\nalong the Eastern Mountains into the I-25 corridor.\nThunderstorms look to fire off by early afternoon across Eastern\nMountains. PWAT values remain high, and this will keep\nprecipitation efficiency high as well. The main concern for\nFriday will be the north to south cell movement, with multiple\nthunderstorms dropping south over the Eastern Mountains, into\nthe I-25 corridor. Flash flooding on Aspen Acres burn scar will\nonce again be possible, and a Flash Flood Watch may be needed.\nGiven the increased shear and modest instability, a few cells\ncould become strong to severe through the afternoon and evening,\nwith hail near 1 inch in diameter and wind gusts near 60 mph.\nHeavy rainfall is expected with thunderstorms. This activity\nwill move southeast off the higher terrain and into the I-25\ncorridor through the evening, clearing into New Mexico\novernight.  \n\nSaturday through Monday...the upper high is forecast to stretch\nfrom southern California, northeast into North Dakota ahead of\nan upper trough over the Pacific Northwest. Drier air will work\ninto the region, with PWAT values falling to well under an inch\nthrough the weekend into Monday. With the upper ridge axis\nmoving overhead, temperatures will warm, with upper 90s to lower\n100s across the Plains, especially Sunday and Monday. Lack of\nembedded energy under the upper ridge should keep shower and\nthunderstorm activity to a minimum. Saturday may see the\ngreatest coverage, with isolated to scattered activity across\nthe higher terrain, with Sunday and Monday likely being mostly\ndry.  \n\nTuesday into Thursday...the upper trough is forecast to move out\nof the Pacific Northwest and across the Northern Plains. A cold\nfront arrives on Tuesday morning, with low level flow easterly,\nand advecting moisture back west across the Plains. Multiple\nshortwave troughs in the westerly flow across Colorado will \nlikely lead to several rounds of showers and thunderstorms for \nmid week, with a return of flash flood potential for the area \nburn scars.",
